$solutionguide = '<p><b>Step 1 — Use the formula.</b> Chebyshev\'s Rule says at least `1 - 1/k^2` of the data lies within `k` standard deviations of the mean, whatever the shape of the distribution.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — Substitute each value of k.</b></p>
<p>(a) `k = ' . $k1 . '`: `1 - 1/' . $k1 . '^2 = 1 - 1/' . ($k1*$k1) . ' = ' . round($p1,4) . '`, which is ' . $d1 . '%.</p>
<p>(b) `k = ' . $k2 . '`: `1 - 1/' . $k2 . '^2 = 1 - 1/' . ($k2*$k2) . ' = ' . round($p2,4) . '`, which is ' . $d2 . '%.</p>
<p>(c) `k = ' . $k3 . '`: `1 - 1/' . $k3 . '^2 = 1 - 1/' . round($k3*$k3,4) . ' = ' . round($p3,4) . '`, which is ' . $d3 . '%.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — Interpret the word "at least".</b> The rule is a floor, not a measurement. It promises that no fewer than ' . $d1 . '% of the observations fall within ' . $k1 . ' standard deviations of the mean. The actual share can be larger — for a bell-shaped distribution about 95% lies within 2 standard deviations, well above the guaranteed 75%. So the classmate is wrong: the rule gives a minimum, not an exact percent.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> (a) ' . $d1 . '% &nbsp; (b) ' . $d2 . '% &nbsp; (c) ' . $d3 . '% &nbsp; (d) the rule gives a minimum percent, and the true percent could be higher.</p>';

